  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this morning shows a weak, elongated trough axis \nover the eastern Plains. This feature is becoming sheared out, stuck \nbetween a building ridge over the High Plains and the deep upper low \nfueling the nor'easter off the Mid-Atlantic coast. We still have \nsome scattered showers across the area this morning in the vicinity \nof the trough axis. These showers should gradually taper off \nthroughout the day, as upper ridging expands eastward and a weak \ninverted surface trough also shifts southeast of the area. Can't rule\nout a brief sprinkle overnight and into tomorrow as easterly low-\nlevel flow keeps some shallow moisture around, but for the most part \nupper ridging should keep us dry through Monday night. With continued\neasterly flow and at least broken cloud cover, highs today and \ntomorrow look to stay generally in the mid/upper 70s.\n\nThe focus of the forecast continues to be on heavy rain potential \nTuesday into Thursday. A deep upper low will strengthen over the \nSouthwest early this week, picking up Hurricane Polo and bringing \nits remnants northeast. Even without the hurricane remnants, the \npath of the strong upper low would be favorable for deep moisture \ntransport from the south. Guidance continues to indicate a prolonged \nperiod of PWAT approaching 2\", supportive of very heavy rain when \ncombined with strong synoptic ascent. The main question is where the \nbetter synoptic ascent will end up. Current guidance is hinting that \nthe heavier rain may come in two semi-distinct rounds. The first of \nthese looks to be Tuesday into early Wednesday, more associated with \nthe remnants of Polo as it lifts north-northeast over western TX and \nKS. The heaviest rain (an axis of 2-5\") with this round is trending \nslightly farther northwest, closer to the main surface low. However \nit still looks close enough to bring moderate to heavy rain (perhaps \n1-3\") to portions of the area, particularly towards central KS.\n\nThe second round then quickly follows later Wednesday into Thursday \nnight with the main upper low. This looks like the best potential to \nbring heavy rain to the entire forecast area, particularly with a \nsoutheastward moving cold front providing additional forcing for \nascent. Potential rain totals across the week haven't changed much. \nHigh confidence in over 1\", with LREF 1\" probabilities over 90 \npercent area-wide. Ensemble mean QPF in the 2-2.5\" range seems \nreasonable for a most-likely amount, while 90th percentiles still \nsuggest some areas may see 4-6\". Given high dewpoints and persistent \ncloud cover, expecting warm overnight lows and cool daytime highs to \ncontinue through Thursday. By Friday into Saturday, precipitation \nchances drop off as we see drier and slightly cooler air move in \nbehind the front.",
